//! Trained weights for Cobo-Briesewitz's own NN-augmentation network, extracted verbatim from
//! `~/Desktop/NA-INDI-firmware/src/modules/src/controller/nn.c` (a file whose own header marks
//! it "GENERATED FILE FROM MODEL ./models/mlp.pth"). Extracted programmatically (parsed the C
//! initializer directly, not retyped by hand) to eliminate transcription error in ~1830 numbers.
//!
//! This is a REAL trained network, not a placeholder: X_mins[15..19]/X_maxs[15..19] run
//! 0..~65500, matching `motorsGetRatio()`'s 16-bit PWM range exactly, and the weight/bias values
//! are ordinary trained floats, not zeros or synthetic patterns.
//!
//! It solves a DIFFERENT problem than this thesis's Strategy 2 (Neural-Swarm2): 19 inputs are
//! this vehicle's OWN state only (rotation matrix columns, EKF acceleration, velocity, gyro,
//! motor PWM ratios) -- no relative/neighbour state at all. It was trained on their own
//! single-vehicle payload-swing experiment. Faithfully porting it (this file + `naindi_hybrid.rs`)
//! makes `controller=8` a genuine, flyable "NA-INDI exactly as published" reference -- it does
//! NOT by itself demonstrate compensation of inter-vehicle downwash, which is what this thesis
//! compares. See `naindi_hybrid.rs`'s own module doc for the full scoping note.
//!
//! Layout, from `nn.h`/`nn.c`: 19 -> 24 -> 24 -> 24 -> 6, LeakyReLU (slope 0.01) on the first
//! three layers, linear on the last. Weight matrices are stored `[n_in][n_out]` (row = input
//! index), matching `nn_utils.c`'s `layer()`: `out[o] = sum_i in[i] * W[i][o] + b[o]` -- this is
//! the TRANSPOSE of PyTorch's `nn.Linear` convention, and indexing it the wrong way round would
//! silently produce a plausible wrong answer rather than a crash.

